qa-sentinels (1.4.2) jammy; urgency=medium

  * Ingest an optional external crash feed, auto-detected from an explicit
    `QA_CRASH_FEED` path, an active faultlab configuration, or an existing
    producer feed file. QA Sentinels does not require faultlab and does not
    probe the feed paths when no producer is configured. A byte cursor and
    fingerprint upsert prevent replay and duplicates.
  * Store the resolved `crashes.component` label at ingest. Fault-core
    crashes with no package owner become `CPU / platform`, kernel MCE/oops
    becomes `linux (Ubuntu)`, and known processes/modules keep their project
    label.
  * Show the stored component in crash queries, reports and fault-core tables;
    prefer it in ticket component selection.
  * Preserve the producer's bounded crash context (trigger lines and coredump
    stack) without the coredump environment block or command line.
  * Add feed, cursor, component and ticket-label tests; document the optional
    feed and `QA_CRASH_FEED` / `QA_CRASH_FEED_USER` (legacy
    `QA_FAULTLAB_CRASH_*` aliases).
  * Surface non-critical kernel watch findings: stale eBPF builds, NOHZ
    tick-stop warnings, disabled KHO, Bluetooth hci0 firmware errors, and
    NVIDIA DRM flip timeouts. Repeated events are grouped at warning severity.

 -- Gerard Sala <creative.gsp@gmail.com>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 17:30:00 +0100

qa-sentinels (1.4.1) jammy; urgency=medium

  * Make component inference total and evidence-first: faulting module
    project, process/executable, event unit, finding rule/subject, then
    `unassigned`. `unknown` is no longer produced, including for locally
    classified findings.
  * Resolve ownership content fallbacks against the crash header and the
    faulting frame only, never the module dump. Python and Electron crashes
    that load `libsystemd`, `libdbus` or `libnvidia` no longer become systemd
    or NVIDIA tickets.
  * Keep vendor tracker components real: Mozilla clamps GTK/X11 and cairo
    frames to Bugzilla components, GNOME uses its project names, and the
    local queue uses descriptive labels such as `python3.10` or
    `smartmontools`.
  * Recognise `org.gnome.*` unit names in the GNOME content rule, so shell
    and session journal events are not left ownerless.
  * Add tests for module-dump isolation, totality, finding/event labels and
    wrong-vendor independence; document the chain in `docs/TICKETS.md`.
  * Support systemd 259 alongside Noble's 255 on the 249+ baseline. On
    systemd 250+ (all later releases), assume the SysV generator is
    unavailable: ignore `LSB:` service failures such as openipmi and record
    them as `sysv-failed/` in inventory. Jammy (systemd 249) keeps the
    SysV/openipmi path. No Ubuntu release version is needed at install time.
  * Match legal-register minimums numerically, so Noble's Python 3.12 and
    SQLite 3.45 satisfy `3.10+` and `3.37+` without warnings.

 -- Gerard Sala <creative.gsp@gmail.com>  Tue, 17 Nov 2026 19:55:00 +0100

qa-sentinels (1.4.0) jammy; urgency=medium

  * Route crashes by faulting module and crashing process instead of by any
    library name in the core: GNOME Shell embeds SpiderMonkey (`libmozjs`), so
    its stacks no longer become Mozilla tickets, and Firefox stacks no longer
    become GNOME tickets from GTK frames. Faults inside the NVIDIA driver are
    still routed to NVIDIA.
  * Derive the ticket component from the faulting frame and process instead of
    a vendor-wide constant: mutter, gjs, gnome-terminal, gnome-software,
    WebRender, JavaScript Engine, or Firefox :: Untriaged.
  * Keep the crashing stack in the draft: drop the `Found module` dump, name
    the faulting frame past the process's SIGSEGV handler, list crash-thread
    modules and state when a symbol is only the nearest export.
  * Select reproduction steps by component: GNOME session/extension steps for
    mutter and gjs, Firefox steps only for Firefox crashes, and no page-load
    guess for events.
  * Claim the fault-core correlation only when a row's own affinity included
    the fault CPUs, not because the vendor rule matched a shared library.
  * Stop redaction from turning `Core :: Graphics` into `Core <ip> Graphics`
    and systemd unit names such as `org.gnome.Shell@x11.service` into
    `<email>`.
  * Rename the vendor prefix and rewrite the manifest when an existing staged
    ticket is re-routed.
  * Add `docs/TICKETS.md` and tests for ownership, component choice, crash
    digests and redaction.

 -- Gerard Sala <creative.gsp@gmail.com>  Tue, 17 Nov 2026 19:12:33 +0100

qa-sentinels (1.3.0) jammy; urgency=medium

  * Add the network and connectivity tier: interfaces, addresses, routes,
    policy rules, DNS/resolved state, Netplan file drift, NetworkManager
    profiles, Wi-Fi signal, WireGuard/OpenVPN/tailscale state, firewall
    summary, conntrack, neighbours, socket counters and the net sysctl tree.
  * Fix listener collection to key on the local address instead of Send-Q, and
    move listening sockets out of the software inventory.
  * Redact IPv6 addresses and network item keys in evidence bundles, and hash
    SSIDs, BSSIDs, WireGuard keys and peer endpoints.
  * Add `qa network collect|show|check`, query categories, report section,
    retention and tests.
  * Summarize retired or offline fault CPUs as "Fault Injection CPUs
    Offline" without listing the unavailable CPUs, omit the fault-core
    correlation section, and state an orientative reproduction viability
    percentage in ticket drafts.
  * Use the configured faultlab CPU set in correlation headings instead of
    a fixed core number.
  * Honor manual ticket bucket moves: reconciliation and staging leave the
    ticket where the operator put it and do not recreate a draft copy.

